  "Number": 13,
  "Question": "The symbol \\(|a|\\) means \\(a\\) if \\(a\\) is a positive number or zero, and \\(-a\\) if \\(a\\) is a negative number. For all real values of \\(t\\) the expression \\(\\sqrt{t^4+t^2}\\) is equal to:",
  "Question Type": "text",
  "Choices": {
    "A": "\\(t^3\\)",
    "B": "\\(t^2+t\\)",
    "C": "\\(|t^2+t|\\)",
    "D": "\\(t\\sqrt{t^2+1}\\)",
    "E": "\\(|t|\\sqrt{1+t^2}\\)"
  },
  "Choices Type": "text",
  "Correct Answer": "E",
  "Solution": "The two terms under the radical share a factor of \\(t^2\\). Factoring exposes a square whose nonnegative square root is \\(|t|\\). For every real \\(t\\), both \\(t^2\\) and \\(t^2+1\\) are nonnegative, so the square-root product rule gives\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\sqrt{t^4+t^2}\n&=\\sqrt{t^2(t^2+1)}\\\\\n&=\\sqrt{t^2}\\sqrt{t^2+1}\\\\\n&=|t|\\sqrt{1+t^2}.\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\nThe absolute value ensures the result is nonnegative even when \\(t<0\\); the identity also holds at \\(t=0\\). Thus the answer is choice E.",
